  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Hiring a Contractor for Bathroom Floor Tile: How to Evaluate Your Subfloor",
    "text": "Hiring a Contractor for Bathroom Floor Tile: How to Evaluate Your Subfloor",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Question: How do I know if my bathroom floor is ready for new tile, or if the subfloor needs replacement first?\n\nAnswer: Ceramic and porcelain floor tiles require a rigid, flat, and dry foundation. If your existing floor has a springy bounce when walked on, noticeable dips, or water damage around the toilet flange and tub edge, setting tile directly on top will lead to cracked grout lines and loose tiles within months.\n\nWhat to Expect During Subfloor Evaluation\nA proper inspection looks closely at the subfloor thickness, joist spacing, and surface deflection. Standard construction often relies on single-layer plywood or oriented strand board, which flexes too much for brittle tile. Professional tile installation requires securing a cement backer board or an uncoupling membrane with thinset and mechanical fasteners, smoothing out high spots, and ensuring the subfloor meets strict flatness standards. \n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nIf you see signs of prior moisture damage, swelling, or rotten wood under an old vanity, your subfloor must be repaired or reinforced before anyone mixes mortar. Skipping subfloor stabilization is the single most common reason bathroom floor tile jobs fail early.\n\nFor a reliable subfloor evaluation and clean bathroom floor tile installation in Aurora, Denver, or surrounding communities, call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
